(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-pulmonary-vascular-disorders-quiz"]={"title":"Pulmonary Vascular Disorders Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why is early pulmonary hypertension easy to miss in an otherwise active adult?","choices":["Symptoms seem like deconditioning","It begins with syncope at rest","It first causes abdominal bloating","It presents with orthopnea"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Exertional dyspnea and fatigue are often the only early symptoms of pulmonary hypertension, and they are frequently dismissed or misattributed to deconditioning. Recognizing that pattern in adults is the chance to find the disease before syncope or right heart failure appears. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 21, 23"},{"q":"How does the right ventricle first adapt to a gradual pressure rise, as in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease?","choices":["Late chamber dilation","Concentric hypertrophy","Acute chamber dilation","Myocardial microinfarction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A gradual pressure rise in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease or interstitial lung disease allows time for compensatory right ventricular hypertrophy: the walls thicken to maintain flow until eventual exhaustion. Chronic cor pulmonale shows concentric hypertrophy followed by late dilation. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 8, 9"},{"q":"Which test proves ventricular stretch and helps separate cardiogenic dyspnea from pulmonary mimics?","choices":["D-dimer assay","Pulmonary function tests","B-type natriuretic peptide","Serum troponin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. B-type natriuretic peptide proves ventricular stretch and volume overload, and a substantial elevation differentiates cardiogenic dyspnea from pulmonary mimics. Heart failure shows a highly elevated level, while a chronic obstructive pulmonary disease exacerbation usually has a normal one. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 36, 43, 54, 58"},{"q":"What defines massive (high-risk) pulmonary embolism?","choices":["Pleuritic pain with hemoptysis","A Wells score above 4","Sustained hypotension","Normotension with high troponin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Massive, high-risk embolism is defined by sustained hypotension (systolic blood pressure below 90 for more than 15 minutes), pulselessness or profound bradycardia, with circulatory collapse. It carries high mortality and requires immediate advanced intervention. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 50, 53, 58"},{"q":"Which diuresis strategy suits an elderly patient with heart failure with preserved ejection fraction?","choices":["Aggressive volume clearance","Deferring diuretics","Fluid boluses for preload","Gentle, precise diuresis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The elderly phenotype (preserved ejection fraction) has age-related myocardial stiffening, elevated end-diastolic pressure, extreme sensitivity to preload changes and a narrow therapeutic margin. It requires gentle, precise diuresis: over-diuresis causes precipitous hypotension, while under-diuresis causes recurrent flooding of the lungs. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"In acute cor pulmonale, what does the septum bowing leftward do to the left ventricle?","choices":["Raises its stroke volume","Reduces its diastolic filling","Increases its diastolic filling","Weakens its contractility"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. When a massive obstruction spikes right ventricular wall tension, the right ventricle dilates and the shared interventricular septum bows leftward into the left ventricle. Left ventricular diastolic distensibility and filling drop drastically, so left ventricular output plummets, causing systemic hypotension and collapse. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 10, 20"},{"q":"When the Wells score is above 4, what is the next step?","choices":["Proceed directly to imaging","Obtain a D-dimer first","Apply the rule-out criteria","Obtain an echocardiogram"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A Wells score above 4 makes pulmonary embolism likely, so the D-dimer is bypassed and the patient goes directly to computed tomographic pulmonary angiography, or to a ventilation-perfusion scan if contrast is contraindicated. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 53, 54, 56, 57"},{"q":"Which statement about dyspnea in proven pulmonary embolism is accurate?","choices":["About 25% lack it","Nearly every patient has it","It marks a massive clot","It develops over several days"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Approximately 25% of patients with proven pulmonary embolism completely lack dyspnea, which is why shortness of breath cannot be relied on. When it does present as isolated dyspnea, the onset is sudden, over minutes to hours, at rest or solely with exertion. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"In acute dyspnea, which finding favors pulmonary embolism over cardiogenic pulmonary edema?","choices":["Hypercapnia with hyperinflation","Clear lungs on chest film","Highly elevated natriuretic peptide","Kerley B lines on film"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonary embolism, the blocked pipe, often presents with clear lungs on the chest film, a normal natriuretic peptide initially, and an S1Q3T3 pattern or right ventricular strain. Heart failure is distinguished by a highly elevated natriuretic peptide, echocardiographic abnormalities and Kerley B lines. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which pulmonary embolism risk category is normotensive but shows right ventricular dysfunction or elevated troponin?","choices":["Transient, provoked risk","Submassive (intermediate risk)","Low risk","Massive (high risk)"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Submassive, or intermediate-risk, embolism is normotensive (systolic blood pressure above 90) but shows right ventricular dysfunction on echocardiography or computed tomographic pulmonary angiography, or myocardial necrosis (elevated troponin or B-type natriuretic peptide). It carries a high risk of sudden deterioration. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 58, 59"},{"q":"Right ventricular failure caused by left heart failure is classified as what?","choices":["Group 2, not cor pulmonale","Group 3 cor pulmonale","Group 1 cor pulmonale","Group 4, not cor pulmonale"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonary hypertension from left heart failure is group 2, post-capillary disease, and it is explicitly not cor pulmonale. Elevated left-sided filling pressures must be excluded before cor pulmonale can be diagnosed, because cor pulmonale requires a primary lung or pulmonary vascular cause. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 4, 5, 19"},{"q":"Pulmonary hypertension from mitral stenosis falls into which mechanism category?","choices":["Pre-capillary, hypoxic","Post-capillary, passive","Pre-capillary, obstructive","Pre-capillary, proliferative"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mitral stenosis, like heart failure with reduced or preserved ejection fraction, obstructs pulmonary venous outflow and causes group 2 pulmonary hypertension from left heart disease. Its mechanism is passive, and it is post-capillary rather than pre-capillary. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is the only definitive option for pulmonary arterial hypertension refractory to maximal medical therapy?","choices":["Pulmonary thromboendarterectomy","Continuous intravenous prostanoid","Calcium channel blockers","Lung transplantation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. For highly selected patients refractory to maximal medical therapy, or those with pulmonary veno-occlusive disease, lung transplantation remains the only definitive therapeutic option. Continuous intravenous support may serve as a bridge to transplant. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 28, 29, 61"},{"q":"What is the single most important driver of pulmonary hypertension in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease?","choices":["Alveolar hypoxia","Polycythemia","Loss of vascular surface area","Left ventricular failure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Alveolar hypoxia is the single most important driver of pulmonary hypertension in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. Global alveolar hypoxia and hypercapnia trigger diffuse, system-wide pulmonary vasoconstriction, a massive rise in pulmonary vascular resistance and a relentless increase in right ventricular afterload. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 4, 6, 7"},{"q":"In pulmonary arterial hypertension, which patients receive high-dose oral calcium channel blockers?","choices":["Those with rapid decline","Those at intermediate risk","Those who are vasoreactive","Those in functional class IV"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The first decision in the treatment algorithm is vasoreactivity: vasoreactive patients receive high-dose oral calcium channel blockers. Patients who are not vasoreactive go on to risk stratification, which decides between oral combination therapy and parenteral prostanoids. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"In the acute rescue of cardiogenic pulmonary edema, which element is used with caution because of respiratory depression?","choices":["Positive airway pressure","Morphine","Intravenous nitrates","Loop diuretics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Morphine is used with caution: historically given for venodilation and anxiolysis, it carries respiratory depression risks. In flash pulmonary edema, too, morphine is given cautiously because it eases anxiety but risks severe reduction in ventilatory drive. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 39, 40, 46"},{"q":"Which pulmonary embolism syndrome indicates a massive, proximal occlusion?","choices":["Circulatory collapse","Isolated dyspnea","Pleuritic pain with hemoptysis","Unilateral leg swelling"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Circulatory collapse, with syncope, severe hypotension and obstructive shock, indicates a massive, proximal occlusion. The other syndromes are isolated dyspnea of sudden onset and pleuritic pain with hemoptysis from a distal, pleural-based infarction. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 48, 50, 53"},{"q":"Why does the right ventricle tolerate a sudden rise in afterload poorly?","choices":["It is thick-walled and stiff","It normally works at high pressure","It hypertrophies within minutes","It is thin-walled and compliant"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The right ventricle is intrinsically a thin-walled, high-compliance, low-pressure chamber that is highly sensitive to sudden afterload changes. In a massive pulmonary embolism, pressure outpaces adaptation: wall tension rises instantly, causing acute dilation, stretch and microinfarction. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 8, 9"},{"q":"Which finding makes a patient fail the PERC (Pulmonary Embolism Rule-out Criteria)?","choices":["Saturation above 94%","Age below 50 years","Exogenous estrogen use","Heart rate below 100"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. All eight PERC (Pulmonary Embolism Rule-out Criteria) items must be true to stop the workup: age under 50, heart rate under 100, oxygen saturation above 94%, no hemoptysis, no exogenous estrogen, no prior venous thromboembolism, no surgery or trauma and no unilateral leg swelling. Estrogen use fails the rule. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Why does advanced pulmonary hypertension cause exertional syncope despite normal left ventricular contractility?","choices":["Weak left ventricular contraction","Rising left ventricular preload","Falling right ventricular afterload","Impaired left ventricular filling"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In ventricular interdependence, the dilated, pressure-overloaded right ventricle bows the septum into the left ventricle. This septal bowing impairs left ventricular diastolic filling, reducing systemic cardiac output and causing exertional syncope even though left ventricular contractility is normal. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 20, 21"},{"q":"In group 3 pulmonary hypertension from chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, which therapy directly reverses hypoxic vasoconstriction?","choices":["Aggressive loop diuresis","Endothelin receptor antagonist","Continuous supplemental oxygen","Phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitor"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Oxygen is a drug: in group 3 pulmonary hypertension from chronic obstructive pulmonary disease or interstitial lung disease, continuous supplemental oxygen is paramount. It directly reverses alveolar hypoxic vasoconstriction and mechanically lowers pulmonary resistance. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 15, 16"},{"q":"Which test confirms pre-capillary pulmonary hypertension?","choices":["Right heart catheterization","Echocardiography","Ventilation-perfusion scan","Pulmonary function testing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Right heart catheterization is the mandatory confirmatory test: it directly measures mean pulmonary artery pressure, pulmonary capillary wedge pressure and pulmonary vascular resistance to confirm pre-capillary pulmonary hypertension definitively. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 24, 13"},{"q":"What is the primary defect in noncardiogenic pulmonary edema?","choices":["Low plasma oncotic pressure","Obstructed pulmonary arteries","Leaking capillary endothelium","Failing left ventricular pump"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Noncardiogenic pulmonary edema is a permeability problem: a shattered capillary endothelium leaks protein-rich fluid, so the primary defect is a leaking pipe. The wedge stays at or below 18 mmHg, unlike the pressure problem of cardiogenic edema. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 32, 33, 43"},{"q":"In an elderly patient with suspected pulmonary embolism and a Wells score of 4 or less, what follows from applying the PERC (Pulmonary Embolism Rule-out Criteria)?","choices":["Stop the workup","Proceed straight to imaging","Repeat the Wells score","Obtain a D-dimer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The PERC (Pulmonary Embolism Rule-out Criteria) are passed only when all eight are true, and one of them is age under 50, so an elderly patient fails the rule. With the Wells score at 4 or less, a failed rule leads to a D-dimer: a negative result rules embolism out, a positive one proceeds to computed tomographic pulmonary angiography. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 54, 57"},{"q":"Which activity advice fits an adult with pulmonary hypertension?","choices":["Strict bedrest at home","Rest until fatigue resolves","Unsupervised sprint training","Supervised aerobic rehabilitation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Strict bedrest is avoided: regular, steady aerobic exercise in a supervised rehabilitation program is safe, prevents muscle deconditioning and improves fatigue. It sits alongside careful diuresis, oxygen during sleep or exertion and annual influenza and pneumococcal immunization. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which Starling force is the engine of cardiogenic pulmonary edema?","choices":["Plasma oncotic pressure","Shattered capillary endothelium","Low capillary hydrostatic pressure","Capillary hydrostatic pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Elevated capillary hydrostatic pressure is the outward force and the engine of cardiogenic edema. Plasma oncotic pressure is the failing inward counterbalance, and the semi-permeable capillary membrane stays intact but overwhelmed; edema follows when hydrostatic pressure exceeds oncotic pressure. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 32, 33"},{"q":"Why is aggressive diuresis dangerous in right ventricular failure from cor pulmonale?","choices":["It causes hyperkalemia","It starves the left ventricle","It worsens hepatic congestion","It raises pulmonary resistance"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The damaged right ventricle relies on elevated filling pressures (preload) to push blood past the high pulmonary resistance. Lowering right ventricular preload too aggressively starves the left ventricle of blood, causing a disastrous drop in systemic cardiac output; hypokalemia can also trigger lethal arrhythmias. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 16, 27"},{"q":"Which symptom is often the only early symptom of pulmonary hypertension?","choices":["Syncope on exertion","Early satiety","Lightheadedness","Exertional dyspnea"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Exertional dyspnea, with fatigue, is often the only early symptom of pulmonary hypertension (World Health Organization class I to II), and it is frequently dismissed or misattributed to deconditioning. Dizziness, syncope and abdominal bloating mark later, more limiting stages. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What is the Palla sign on a chest film in pulmonary embolism?","choices":["Focal peripheral oligemia","Central perihilar edema","Enlarged right descending artery","Wedge-shaped pleural opacity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The Palla sign is an enlarged right descending pulmonary artery. With the Westermark sign and Hampton's hump it is a classic chest film clue, highly suggestive of pulmonary embolism but infrequent; most films in embolism are abnormal but nonspecific. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 51, 37"},{"q":"After a pulmonary embolism provoked by recent surgery, when may stopping anticoagulation be considered?","choices":["After 1 month","After 3 months","After 6 months","After 12 months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Anticoagulation starts at diagnosis, and 3 months is the decision point. With a transient, provoked risk such as recent surgery, trauma, hospitalization or estrogen therapy, stopping after 3 months is considered; a persistent or unprovoked risk favors indefinite anticoagulation. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 59"}]};
